# --- Top level: track nothing but the figures pipeline (for now) ---
/*
!/.gitignore
!/README.md
!/figures/

# --- Bulk data: regenerable, LFS-scale ---
figures/trends-figure/data/raw/**/*.xml
figures/trends-figure/data/raw/**/progress.jsonl
figures/trends-figure/data/interim/

# --- Large per-record intermediates (aggregated tables are kept) ---
figures/trends-figure/data/processed/pattern_hits.csv
figures/trends-figure/data/processed/paper_labels.csv
figures/trends-figure/data/processed/synthetic/paper_labels.csv

# --- Run noise and scratch ---
figures/trends-figure/logs/
figures/trends-figure/figure_mockup.jpg
figures/clinical-operations/logs/

# --- clinical-operations: raw snapshots ARE tracked ---
# Unlike trends-figure's ~1 GB of PubMed XML, this project's raw data is about
# 1.1 MB per snapshot: one FDA CSV and four openFDA batch responses. Keeping
# them in the repository makes the figure reproducible from a clone with no
# network access, which matters because FDA republishes its device list in place
# at a fixed URL and does not version it. Nothing to ignore here on purpose.

# --- Large derived exports: keep the source, rebuild the export ---
# Each is regenerable from the SVG source tracked beside it, and together they
# come to 91 MB - the multimodal grid PDF alone is 52 MB, over GitHub's 50 MB
# per-file warning. Nothing is lost by excluding them:
#   figure1-combo.pdf  ->  cd figures/figure1-combo && python3 build_pdf.py
#                          (read its header first; a plain rsvg-convert or
#                           ImageMagick call silently drops the pathology
#                           rasters and shifts the type ladder)
#   multimodal grid    ->  export the tracked .svg from your SVG tool
figures/figure1-combo/figure1-combo.pdf
figures/figure1-combo/figure1-combo.png
figures/multimodal_integration_figure_inkstyle_grid.pdf
figures/multimodal_integration_figure_inkstyle_grid.png

# --- Virtual environments ---
# These belong OUTSIDE the repository: the whole tree is OneDrive-synced and a
# venv inside it pushes tens of thousands of files into cloud sync. One was
# created at figures/clinical-operations/.venv on 2026-09-03 and reached 353 MB
# before it was removed. Use ~/.venvs/<project> instead; reproducibility rests
# on the pinned requirements files.
.venv/
venv/

# --- Caches ---
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/
.DS_Store
